  DOES THE CODE HOLD DOWN THE LINEAGE? generation 4 minus generation 0,
  paired per lineage. tutorwide's TWO-signal code read 1.4 SE here.
    bit F1   -21.58 +/-  5.56  (3.9 SE)  a RESOLVED loss
    bit F2    +0.97 +/- 27.91  (0.0 SE)  no detectable loss
  A loss under 3 SE is NOT proof the code holds -- it is a failure to
  resolve one, and at n=18 the smallest loss this could resolve is about
  17 and 84 Hz on the two bits. Read it as a BOUND on the decay.

  INHERITANCE -- the flip chain must ALTERNATE, lineage by lineage
    bit F1 alternates in 18 of 18 lineages
    bit F2 alternates in 16 of 18 lineages
    A re-imposed code holds one sign; an inherited one flips with the tape.

  THE CODE DOES NOT CROSS AT FOUR SIGNALS. Generation 0 produces it on its
  own index (+37.6/+91.9 Hz) and generation 4 does not (+16.1 +/- 5.5 and
  +92.9 +/- 28.1). Transmission at TWO signals is established at no loss over
  four generations, so this is a VOCABULARY limit in the channel rather than
  a channel that leaks: what a pupil can recover from a tutor's four-way
  production is less than what it can recover from a two-way one.
